## Assembling the pieces

You've now met every ingredient of a <GlossaryPopover term="transformer">transformer</GlossaryPopover>: token vectors (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), layers of
neurons (<ChapterRef to="a-neuron" />), and attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />). A transformer arranges them into a **block** and stacks that
block many times.<Cite id="vaswani2017" /> Build GPT-2 small one piece at a time:

<BlockBuilder />

<At levels="simple">

Two pieces are new. The "+" circles mean attention and the neuron layer (the **MLP**) add a change
to each token's vector instead of replacing it, so the original passes straight through on a
highway.<Cite id="he2016" /> **Layer norm** rescales the numbers to a steady size before each
step.<Cite id="ba2016" /> Both make big networks much easier to train.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Two pieces are new:

- **The "+" circles.** Attention and the neuron layer (called the **MLP**) don't replace a token's
  vector; they add a change to it. The original passes straight through on a highway, and each
  block adds its contribution. This trick, called a residual connection, makes very deep networks
  much easier to train.<Cite id="he2016" />
- **Layer norm.** Before each step, the numbers are rescaled to a steady size, which also helps
  training stay stable.<Cite id="ba2016" /> GPT-2 puts it at the start of each step, plus once more
  at the very end.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### A block, written down

With $x$ a token's vector on the highway, one GPT-2 block computes

$$
x' = x + \operatorname{Attn}\!\left(\operatorname{LN}(x)\right), \qquad x'' = x' + \operatorname{MLP}\!\left(\operatorname{LN}(x')\right)
$$

where $\operatorname{Attn}$ is the attention from <ChapterRef to="attention" />, $\operatorname{MLP}$
is the two layers of neurons from <ChapterRef to="a-neuron" />, and $x''$ goes on to the next
block. Layer norm rescales the 768 numbers of a vector by their own mean $\mu$ and variance
$\sigma^2$:

$$
\operatorname{LN}(x) = \gamma \odot \frac{x - \mu}{\sqrt{\sigma^2 + \epsilon}} + \beta
$$

where $\gamma$ and $\beta$ are 768 learned numbers each, $\odot$ multiplies number by number, and
$\epsilon$ is a tiny constant (0.00001 in GPT-2) that avoids dividing by zero.<Cite id="ba2016" /><Cite id="gpt2-config" />
The original transformer put the norm after each addition instead, as
$\operatorname{LN}(x + \operatorname{Attn}(x))$; GPT-2 moved it to the start of each
step.<Cite id="vaswani2017" /><Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>

<At levels="simple">

The counter shows where GPT-2's 124 million weights live: most are in the token table at the start
and in the MLPs. Attention, the star of the last chapter, has fewer than a quarter.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The counter shows where GPT-2's 124 million weights live. About a third are in the token and
position tables at the start. Almost half are in the MLPs, which take up two thirds of every block.
Attention, the star of the last chapter, has fewer than a quarter.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

Counted exactly, each block has 2,362,368 weights in attention ($4 \times 768^2$ plus $4 \times 768$
biases), 4,722,432 in the MLP, and 3,072 in its two layer norms: 7,087,872 in all. Adding the token
table (50,257 × 768), the position table (1,024 × 768), the 12 blocks, and the final layer norm
(2 × 768) gives the total on the counter:

$$
50{,}257 \times 768 + 1{,}024 \times 768 + 12 \times 7{,}087{,}872 + 2 \times 768 = 124{,}439{,}808
$$

There's no separate output table: the last step reuses the token table, so it isn't counted
twice.

</At>

## Watch a guess take shape

<At levels="simple">

That highway is called the <GlossaryPopover term="residual-stream">residual stream</GlossaryPopover>.
Every block reads from it and writes back to it, and the final stream becomes the prediction. You
can also stop partway and ask for a prediction from the middle, a trick called the logit
lens.<Cite id="nostalgebraist2020" /> Here it is on "The cat sat on the":

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

That highway is called the <GlossaryPopover term="residual-stream">residual stream</GlossaryPopover>. Every block reads from
it and writes back to it, and the final stream becomes the prediction. So what if you stopped early
and asked for a prediction from the middle of the stack? Researchers do exactly that, a trick
called the logit lens.<Cite id="nostalgebraist2020" /> Here it is on "The cat sat on the":

</At>

<ResidualStream />

<At levels="simple">

Early on, the top guess is " same", as in "sat on the same": a sensible kind of word, but not about
cats. Later, " floor" rises to the top and furniture appears. The last block ends at " floor",
" bed", and " couch", the guesses from <ChapterRef to="guessing-game" />.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

At the start, the stream is just the vectors for " the" and its position, and read as a guess it's
nonsense. From block 1 to block 7 the top guess is " same", as in "sat on the same": a sensible kind
of word, but not about cats. From block 8 on, " floor" is at or near the top (" ground" edges ahead
once), and furniture appears: " table", " sofa", " bed". The last block spreads the bets out again,
ending at " floor", " bed", and " couch", the same guesses you saw in <ChapterRef to="guessing-game" />.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

The lens reads block $\ell$'s stream $x_\ell$ the way the model reads its last one: apply the
final layer norm, then score every token against the token table $W_E$ and take softmax,

$$
p_\ell = \operatorname{softmax}\!\left(\operatorname{LN}_f(x_\ell)\, W_E^{\top}\right)
$$

where $\operatorname{LN}_f$ is the final layer norm and $W_E^{\top}$ is the token table turned on
its side, so each token's score is its vector times the stream.<Cite id="nostalgebraist2020" />
The lens is only a rough view: the middle blocks weren't trained to be read this way.

</At>

No single block "knows" the answer. Each one nudges the stream, and the prediction sharpens as it
goes.

<RecapCard>

- A transformer block is attention plus an MLP, each added onto the residual stream, with layer
  norm before each.
- GPT-2 small stacks 12 blocks. Most of its weights are in the MLPs and the token table, not in
  attention.
- Each block makes a small change; reading the stream partway through shows the guess forming.

<At levels="deep">

- A block is $x + \operatorname{Attn}(\operatorname{LN}(x))$, then the same with the MLP: 7,087,872
  weights each in GPT-2.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** the model gives probabilities for one next token. How does that become a whole
paragraph?
